  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Nowruz, the 3,000-year-old Persian New Year, heralds the arrival of spring and the vernal equinox (typically March 20-21). Celebrated by over 300 million people, it symbolizes renewal, rejuvenation, and the triumph of light over darkness, marking the first day of the Iranian calendar. It is marked by family gatherings, street festivals, and setting the \"Haft-Sin\" table, which holds seven symbolic items representing nature. Key Traditions and Significance Significance: It is a secular holiday rooted in Zoroastrianism, representing a fresh start and the rebirth of nature. Haft-Sin Table: Families set a table with seven items starting with the Persian letter 'S' (seen). These typically include Sabzeh (wheat sprouts for renewal), Samanu (sweet pudding for strength), Senjed (dried fruit for love), Seer (garlic for health), Seeb (apple for beauty), Somāq (sumac for sunrise), and Serkeh (vinegar for patience). Preparation: Ahead of the new year, people clean their homes (khooneh takouni) and jump over fires during Chaharshanbe Suri to burn away negativity. Celebration & Foods: Celebrations last 13 days, concluding on Sizdebedar (April 1 or 2), a day spent picnicking outdoors to avoid bad luck. Traditional foods include Sabzi Polo ba Mahi (rice with herbs and fish) and Ash-e Reshteh (noodle soup). International Recognition: It is observed in Iran, Azerbaijan, Afghanistan, Central Asia, and by diaspora communities worldwide, recognized by the UN as International Nowruz Day.",
